title: Bookshelf
---
body:

> "Read books are far less valuable than unread ones."
> — Nassim Taleb

Taleb calls this an anti-library: the unread shelf is worth more than the read one, a map of what you do not yet know rather than a trophy case for what you do. 
The Japanese have a word for the habit that builds one, [tsundoku](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsundoku), buying books and letting them stack up unread... 

Read... Not yet read... The stack below is mine... in no particular order or importance...

- **The Goal**, Eliyahu Goldratt (1984). Re-reading now for a review on throughput accounting, the part everyone skips in favour of the bottleneck story.
- **Inventory Analytics**, Riccardo Rossi (2021).
- **I Write What I Like**, Steve Biko (1978). [Review attempt...](/reading/I%20Write%20What%20I%20Like/).
- *Fooled by Randomness*, Nassim Nicholas Taleb
- *The Signal and the Noise*, Nate Silver
- *Struck by Lightning: The Curious World of Probabilities*, Jeffrey S. Rosenthal
- *Thinking, Fast and Slow*, Daniel Kahneman
- *Practical Uncertainty*, Hossein Pishro-Nik
- *The Resilient Enterprise*, Yossi Sheffi
- *The Haystack Syndrome*, Eliyahu Goldratt
- *Throughput Accounting*, Thomas Corbett
- *The Almanack of Naval Ravikant*, Eric Jorgenson

